  "query": "My last three keyboards each died inside two years, one from a stuck key, one from a flaky cable, one from letters wearing blank. This time I want to buy once and be done for a decade. Switches advertise 50 or 100 million keystroke ratings, but I have no idea whether that number means anything in real desks-and-coffee life, or whether it's actually the keycaps, stabilizers, or circuit board that give out first. Which boards do long-term owners say are still going strong after years of daily abuse, and what would you pick as the genuine buy-it-for-life option?",
